    {
      "title": "US reacts cautiously to Myanmar genocide ruling as EU praises ‘critical’ role of UN’s top court",
      "link": "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2026/nov/20/us-eu-reaction-myanmar-rohingya-genocide-icj-ruling-trump-international-law",
      "description": "<p>Reactions to ICJ ruling underline global divisions over court with China raising concerns about judicial overreach</p><p>The US has greeted with caution the world’s highest court’s ruling that <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/nov/19/genocide-rohingya-international-court-of-justice-ruling-myanmar\">Myanmar committed genocide</a> against its Rohingya minority, while China has raised concerns of judicial overreach.</p><p>Reactions to Thursday’s historic ruling underlined deep international division over the role of the court and similar institutions. The international court of justice (ICJ) had never previously found a state responsible for genocide and the decision could set a precedent for future genocide claims, notably a <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/jan/11/icj-case-against-israel-could-finally-empower-the-genocide-convention\">case against Israel</a>.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/law/2026/nov/20/us-eu-reaction-myanmar-rohingya-genocide-icj-ruling-trump-international-law\">Continue reading...</a>",
      "source_name": "The Guardian World",
      "published": "2026-11-20T11:57:43"
    },
    {
      "title": "Bangladesh asks Myanmar to compensate Rohingyas, ensure their safe return",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/world-news/bangladesh-asks-myanmar-to-compensate-rohingyas-ensure-their-safe-return-126112000833_1.html",
      "description": "Bangladesh on Friday called on Myanmar to compensate the Rohingya people for crimes committed against them and ensure their safe, voluntary and dignified return following the International Court of Justice's genocide ruling.\nIn a landmark judgment, the ICJ on Thursday ruled that Myanmar committed \"genocide\" against the minority Rohingya in the northern Rakhine state during the 2017 \"clearance operations\".\nFor the first time in its 80-year history, the ICJ, also dubbed the UN's top court, found a state responsible for committing genocide under the 1948 Genocide Convention and handed down the unprecedented ruling.\nAddressing Rohingya refugees in Cox's Bazar's Ukhiya, Bangladesh's Foreign Minister Khalilur Rahman said that the ICJ's judgment on the atrocities against them in Myanmar is a historic moment and it should mark the beginning of their journey towards justice and a safe return home, the state-run BSS news agency reported.\n\"The judgment is final and binding and cannot be ...",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-20T17:03:05"
    }
